{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To find the constant \\( k \\) such that the graphs of the parabolas \\( y = x^2 + k \\) and \\( x = y^2 + k \\) are tangent to each other, we need to ensure that they intersect at exactly one point and that their slopes are equal at that point.\n\nFirst, let's denote the point of tangency as \\( (a, b) \\). Since the point \\( (a, b) \\) lies on both parabolas, it must satisfy both equations:\n\\[ b = a^2 + k \\]\n\\[ a = b^2 + k \\]\n\nFrom these equations, we can express \\( k \\) in terms of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\):\n\\[ k = b - a^2 \\]\n\\[ k = a - b^2 \\]\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\( k \\), we get:\n\\[ b - a^2 = a - b^2 \\]\nRearranging terms, we have:\n\\[ b - a = a^2 - b^2 \\]\nFactoring the right side, we get:\n\\[ b - a = (a - b)(a + b) \\]\nRewriting, we have:\n\\[ b - a = -(b - a)(a + b) \\]\nIf \\( b \\neq a \\), we can divide both sides by \\( b - a \\):\n\\[ 1 = -(a + b) \\]\nThus:\n\\[ a + b = -1 \\]\nSo, we can express \\( b \\) as:\n\\[ b = -1 - a \\]\n\nNow substitute \\( b = -1 - a \\) back into one of the original equations to find \\( k \\). Using \\( b = a^2 + k \\):\n\\[ -1 - a = a^2 + k \\]\nSolving for \\( k \\), we get:\n\\[ k = -1 - a - a^2 \\]\n\n","prefix_end":1117,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,1477,279,6783,17767,595,1124,8,1741,429,279,38999,315,279,1346,370,19186,17767,379,284,856,61,17,488,595,1124,8,323,17767,856,284,379,61,17,488,595,1124,8,525,68660,311,1817,1008,11,582,1184,311,5978,429,807,31796,518,6896,825,1459,323,429,862,59008,525,6144,518,429,1459,382,5338,11,1077,594,78064,279,1459,315,21878,2251,438,17767,320,64,11,293,8,1124,568,8704,279,1459,17767,320,64,11,293,8,1124,8,15448,389,2176,1346,370,19186,11,432,1969,26553,2176,37906,510,78045,293,284,264,61,17,488,595,1124,921,78045,264,284,293,61,17,488,595,1124,2533,3830,1493,37906,11,582,646,3158